    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{ad-bc}{c^2}=\\dfrac{ps-qr}{r^2}$.\n\nRewrite both as translated reciprocal curves; translations preserve the reciprocal coefficient.",
      "worked": "Rewrite each hyperbola in translated reciprocal form:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=\\frac ac+\\frac{bc-ad}{c(cx+d)}\\\\f(x)&=\\frac ac-\\frac{(ad-bc)/c^2}{x+d/c}\\\\g(x)&=\\frac pr+\\frac{qr-ps}{r(rx+s)}\\\\g(x)&=\\frac pr-\\frac{(ps-qr)/r^2}{x+s/r}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nLet $A=\\dfrac{ad-bc}{c^2}$ and $B=\\dfrac{ps-qr}{r^2}$. A translation with horizontal component $h$ and vertical component $k$ gives\n\n$$\\begin{align*}y-k&=f(x-h)\\\\y&=\\frac ac+k-\\frac A{x-h+d/c}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nChoose $h=\\dfrac dc-\\dfrac sr$ and $k=\\dfrac pr-\\dfrac ac$ to align the asymptotes:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=\\frac pr-\\frac A{x+s/r}\\\\g(x)&=\\frac pr-\\frac B{x+s/r}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThese rules agree exactly when $A=B$. Therefore, the required condition is\n\n$$\\frac{ad-bc}{c^2}=\\frac{ps-qr}{r^2}.$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
